"At the beginning of Surah al-Anbiya, the secret whisperings of the polytheists against our Prophet are recounted. In order to belittle prophecy, they put forward various claims, such as: 'This is a muddled dream,' 'it is a fabrication,' 'it is the word of a poet.' When our Prophet informed them of their secret conversations through the revelation he received from Allah, they were utterly bewildered. Thereupon, they said, 'Let him bring us a miracle.' Yet the prophets were not lifeless statues; they were human beings who ate food. This denier attitude of theirs stemmed from their hearts being in heedlessness.\n\nNext, it is recalled that many towns that were oppressive in the past were destroyed. Even if they tried to flee when they felt the punishment, it is told that they must return to their dwellings and be questioned. They cry out, 'Woe to us, we were truly wrongdoers!' This punishment continues until it turns them into reaped harvest and a heap of ashes. Allah emphasizes that He did not create the heavens and the earth in vain, that had He wished to take an amusement He would have taken it from His own presence, but He did not create anything as a plaything. The truth strikes against falsehood and obliterates it. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah; the angels never grow weary of worshipping Him.\n\nThe polytheists taking gods besides Allah is questioned. If there were gods in the earth and heaven besides Allah, both of them would be corrupted and destroyed. For if there is more than one god, there would be disagreement and conflict between them, which would disrupt the order of the universe. Yet the earth and the heavens stand firm, and this is a clear proof of Allah's oneness. Allah cannot be questioned about what He does, but the servants will be questioned. The disbelievers are told, 'Bring your proof'; they have neither rational nor textual evidence. Yet the Quran and the previous scriptures set forth the belief in tawhid (the oneness of God).\n\nThe common message revealed to all prophets is this: 'There is no god but Me, so worship Me.' The polytheists, on the other hand, slander by saying, 'The Most Merciful has taken a child.' Yet the angels are not Allah's children, but honored servants. They do not precede Allah in speech; they act by His command. Allah knows what is before them and what is behind them; they do not intercede except for someone with whom He is pleased. Whoever says, 'I am a god besides Him,' his recompense is Hell.\n\nDo the disbelievers not see that the heavens and the earth were joined together and then separated, and that every living thing was created from water? Mountains have been placed on the earth lest it shake with the people, and pathways have been made between them. The sky, meanwhile, has been made a protected canopy. It is Allah Who created the night, the day, the sun, and the moon; each travels along an orbit. No human being before you was granted immortality; every soul shall taste death, and will be tested with good and evil. The disbelievers mock the Prophet and ask, 'When is this promise?' Yet the punishment will come upon them suddenly, and they will neither be able to repel it nor will they be granted respite. Messengers before were also mocked, and in the end, the punishment engulfed those who mocked.\n\nEvery soul shall taste death. Allah tests human beings with good and evil. Everything does not end with death; everyone will ultimately be returned to Allah, and their deeds will be weighed. The disbelievers mock the Prophet. Who can protect you night and day from the Most Merciful? The entities they have taken as helpers besides Allah cannot even benefit themselves. Allah granted the disbelievers and their forefathers long lives, yet He diminishes their land from all its borders. The Prophet warns only by revelation; the deaf cannot hear the call. If even a slight touch of the punishment reaches them, they say, 'Woe to us, we were truly wrongdoers!' On the Day of Judgment, scales of justice will be set up; even an act of goodness the weight of a mustard seed will be repaid.\n\nMoses (Musa) and Aaron (Harun) were given the Criterion (al-Furqan, the Torah) as a light and a reminder for the righteous. They fear their Lord unseen and tremble before the Day of Resurrection. The Quran is also a blessed reminder. Earlier, Abraham (Ibrahim) had been granted his right guidance. He asked his father and his people what these idols were that they devoted themselves to. When they said they were following their forefathers, Abraham accused them of being in plain error. He said, 'Your Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth, Who created them.' He broke the idols, leaving only the biggest of them. When his people questioned him, he demonstrated the futility of worshipping idols that cannot speak. They wanted to burn him, but Allah said to the fire, 'Be cool and peaceful.' He delivered Abraham and Lot (Lut) to the holy land. He bestowed upon Abraham Isaac (Ishaq) and Jacob (Ya'qub), and made them leaders. Lot was granted sound judgment and knowledge, and He saved him from the town that practiced vile deeds. Noah (Nuh) had also prayed earlier; his prayer was answered, and he was saved along with his family, while his people were drowned.\n\nRemember David (Dawud) and Solomon (Sulayman). They gave judgment regarding the sheep that strayed and grazed at night in a field. David judged that the sheep should be given to the owner of the field, whereas Solomon deemed it appropriate for the owner of the field to benefit from the sheep's milk until the crops returned to their original state. Allah made the correct judgment known to Solomon. He subjected the mountains and the birds to David, and taught him the art of making coats of mail. To Solomon, He subjected the raging wind and the devils who dived. Job (Ayyub) prayed, 'Affliction has touched me, and You are the Most Merciful of the merciful.' His prayer was answered, his afflictions were removed, and his family was restored to him along with the like of them besides. Ishmael (Isma'il), Idris, and Dhul-Kifl were among the patient; they attained mercy. Jonah (Yunus, Dhu'n-Nun) had departed in anger, but in the darknesses he glorified Allah and confessed that he had been wrongful. His prayer was answered and he was delivered from distress. Zechariah (Zakariyya) pleaded, 'My Lord! Do not leave me alone.' His prayer was answered and he was granted John (Yahya), and his wife was made capable of bearing children. They used to hasten to good deeds and supplicate Us in fear and hope.\n\nMary (Maryam) guarded her chastity; We breathed into her from Our spirit, and made her and her son a sign for all the worlds. Truly this community of yours is a single community, and I am your Lord, so worship Me. But people divided their religion among themselves; all shall return to Us. Whoever does righteous deeds while being a believer, his effort will not be denied, and We record it. It is impossible for the people of any town We destroyed not to return to Us. When the barrier of Gog and Magog (Ya'juj and Ma'juj) is opened, they will descend from every height. When the true promise draws near, the eyes of the disbelievers will stare in horror: 'Woe to us! We were in heedlessness of this; we were wrongdoers,' they will say. You and what you worship besides Allah are the fuel of Hell; you will enter it. If these had been true gods, they would not have entered it; all will abide therein permanently. There they will sigh and groan, and they will hear nothing. Those for whom the best reward has preceded from Us will be kept far away from Hell; they will not hear even its slightest sound, and they will abide eternally in whatever their souls desire. That supreme terror will not grieve them, and the angels will welcome them, saying, 'This is your Day which you were promised.'\n\nWhen We roll up the sky like the rolling up of a scroll, We will recreate it just as We began the first creation; this is Our promise. After the Torah, We also wrote in the Psalms that My righteous servants shall inherit the earth. In this Quran there is sufficient counsel for those who worship. O Muhammad! We have sent you only as a mercy to the worlds. Say: 'It is revealed to me that your God is only one God; will you now become Muslims?' If they turn away, say: 'I have declared it to you openly; I do not know whether that which you are threatened with is near or far. Allah knows both the open and the secret. I do not know; perhaps this delay is to test you and to grant you enjoyment for a time.' The Prophet said: 'O my Lord! Judge with truth; and the Most Compassionate (al-Rahman) is the One whose help is sought against what you ascribe.'"